    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In November 2022, the Governments of Thailand and Australia signed a Memorandum of Understanding to establish Thailand&#x2019;s new Counter Trafficking in Persons Centre of Excellence, the first facility in Southeast Asia with a dedicated focus on combating human trafficking. 
&#x200d;
The Australian Government, through the Australia-ASEAN Counter Trafficking Program (ASEAN-ACT), is providing technical support to assist Thailand to design and operationalise their Centre of Excellence, including through the development of a new national curriculum for counter-trafficking actors with a focus on gender-sensitive, victim-centred approaches.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening ASEAN&#x2019;s response to trafficking in persons
&#x200d;
The ASEAN-Australia Counter Trafficking Program is strengthening justice responses to trafficking while advancing victims&#x2019; rights in the ASEAN region. The program plays a pivotal role in strengthening ASEAN&#x2019;s framework to combat trafficking in persons by enhancing capacity and collaboration among ASEAN bodies to implement the ASEAN Convention Against Trafficking in Persons, Especially Women and Children (&#x2018;the Convention&#x2019;).
&#x200d;
In recognition of the complex nature of trafficking in persons, ASEAN adopted the ASEAN Multi Sectoral Work Plan Against Trafficking in Persons 2023-2028 (Bohol Work Plan 2.0 or the &#x2018;Work Plan&#x2019;) in August 2023. The Work Plan sets out ASEAN&apos;s strategy for progressing the implementation of the Convention and articulates clear roles and responsibilities across the ASEAN Communities and sectoral bodies in combating trafficking in persons.  ASEAN ACT provided technical, facilitative and financial support to ASEAN to develop the Work Plan.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ASEAN Australia Counter Trafficking Program is strengthening justice responses to trafficking in persons in the ASEAN region.  Since 2020, it has supported the development of victim sensitive court indicators across six ASEAN countries to improve handling of trafficking cases. ASEAN-ACT has provided technical, facilitative, and financial assistance to strengthen judicial policies and procedures to increase victims&#x2019; access to justice and minimise further harm.
&#x200d;
In September 2023, Laos became the first ASEAN country to adopt victim sensitive court guideline.  The adoption of the guideline by the People&#x2019;s Supreme Court ensures judges are obligated to sensitively handle trafficking victims &#x2013; for example, ensuring victims are not forced to face perpetrators in court and applying special measures for child victims. ASEAN-ACT trained and supported 15 judges who then institutionalised the guideline into judicial trainings for all judges in Laos.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The development of the ASEAN Guideline on the Implementation of the Non-Punishment Principle for Protection of Victims of Trafficking in Persons was supported by the Australian government through the ASEAN-Australia Counter Trafficking program (ASEAN-ACT). ASEAN- ACT worked in partnership with the ASEAN Senior Officials&#x2019; Meeting on Transnational Crime (SOMTC) and the ASEAN Intergovernmental Commission on Human Rights (AICHR) to develop the Guideline. 
&#x200d;
The Guideline provides practical advice to ASEAN policy makers and practitioners on the application of the non-punishment principle to ensure victims of trafficking are not punished for crimes they committed as result of being trafficked. It represents a major regional milestone in human rights and supports ASEAN Member States to implement their legal obligations as stipulated in the ASEAN Convention Against Trafficking in Persons, especially women and children.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vietnam&#x2019;s revised Trafficking in Person (TIP) law was approved by the National Assembly of Vietnam in November 2024. The update marks a significant step forward to align Vietnam&#x2019;s legislative framework with the ASEAN Convention Against Trafficking in Persons, especially women and children and international standards particularly the UN Palermo Protocol. Australia supported the revision process through a collaboration between the ASEAN-Australia Counter Trafficking program (ASEAN-ACT), the Australian Attorney General&#x2019;s Department and the UN Office on Drugs and Crime. ASEAN-ACT&#x2019;s technical assistance enhanced the capacity of the legal drafting committee and facilitated policy dialogue between government agencies and NGOs.
&#x200d;
Australia&#x2019;s contributions were instrumental in shaping key reforms reflected in the revised law including: 
&#x200d;
- Alignment of trafficking in persons definition with international and regional obligations and clearer definitions of sexual exploitation, forced labour and child trafficking. 
&#x200d;
- Updated definition of child trafficking from age 16 to 18 years. 
&#x200d;
- Integration of the non-punishment principle to provide a legal mandate for the protection of trafficking victims. 
&#x200d;
- Stipulation that presumed victims of trafficking are entitled to support and protection to meet their basic needs including compensation and livelihood support.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
